This program contains the implementation of a Generative Adversarial Network (GAN) designed to learn and generate optimal trajectories for the Zermelo Navigation problem. 
The model is trained on a dataset of 4000 time-optimal trajectories between fixed start and end positions in a 7-dimensional state space. 
The GAN consists of a deep feedforward generator that maps 20-dimensional latent vectors to 175-dimensional trajectory outputs, and a discriminator that classifies trajectories as real or fake. 
Training uses binary cross-entropy loss with stochastic gradient descent and runs on GPU by default. 
After training, the generator can produce synthetic trajectories that mimic the statistical characteristics of the true data. 
The code saves both the trained models and 1000 generated samples to disk for further analysis or downstream tasks.
